{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>As HaShem commanded Moshe.</b> It was necessary to say <b>et Moshe</b>, and it would not have sufficed to say only ״as HaShem commanded״ — for the matter is well known, that it was Moshe whom HaShem commanded? The verse's intention is to inform us that the Torah testifies about the deed that there was no deviation from the commandment, and the force of saying ״as״ applies to the substance of the deed itself. For had it said ״as HaShem commanded him,״ it would have been understood as referring back to the one who performed it — that he did as HaShem commanded him. And understand this. The reason for the repetition of the phrase regarding each and every detail is to show that every single detail is an important commandment in its own right, worthy of having said about it alone ״HaShem commanded Moshe.״"], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 40, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:37:21.642431029Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}